WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.560
The two kinases are on.

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:11.980
They add phosphate to tyrosines outside the kinase core, mostly on the two flexible

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.900
tails. Each of those phosphates is a docking site.

4
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.000
So how does a tail pick its partner?

5
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:23.673
An SH2 domain binds a phosphotyrosine.

6
00:00:24.173 --> 00:00:26.573
It also checks the few residues that follow.

7
00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:29.933
So the sequence decides which protein binds.

8
00:00:31.073 --> 00:00:37.993
On the EGF receptor tail, phosphotyrosine 1068 has an asparagine two residues later.

9
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:44.693
That suits the SH2 domain of Grb2, which leads to Ras and the MAP kinase cascade.

10
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:50.853
Phosphotyrosine 992, on the same tail, binds PLC-gamma.

11
00:00:51.513 --> 00:00:54.293
That enzyme makes IP3 and DAG.

12
00:00:55.167 --> 00:01:02.527
Now look at HER3. Its tail has six tyrosines, and each has a methionine three

13
00:01:02.527 --> 00:01:09.567
residues later. That suits the SH2 domains of PI 3-kinase, which leads to Akt.

14
00:01:10.327 --> 00:01:14.347
So one activated pair starts several pathways at once.

15
00:01:15.187 --> 00:01:17.387
The choice of partner changes the mix.

16
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:21.157
Does each site serve its own protein?

17
00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:27.077
The PDGF receptor carries sites for all three proteins on a single chain.

18
00:01:28.497 --> 00:01:30.797
Researchers tested the sites one at a time.

19
00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:36.257
They changed a tyrosine to phenylalanine, which cannot be phosphorylated.

20
00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:40.497
That protein failed to dock, and the others still did.

21
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:44.397
PI 3-kinase uses two such tyrosines.
